  "prompt_text": "The figure is a schematic cross-section of a developing brain wall organized around a central lumen. It highlights how several progenitor cell types are arranged radially across germinal zones. Closest to the lumen, the ventricular zone (VZ) contains neuroepithelial cells and ventricular radial glia (vRG cells), whose cell bodies sit near the inner surface and extend long processes outward. Farther from the lumen, in the outer subventricular zone (oSVZ), outer radial glia (oRG cells) are shown with elongated basal fibers but without direct attachment to the lumen.\n\nAround the outer edge, many small round blue cells labeled IPC appear dispersed, indicating intermediate progenitor cells located away from the ventricular surface. The overall message is a progression from apically anchored progenitors in the VZ to more basally positioned progenitors in the oSVZ, with distinct morphologies and spatial relationships. The radial arrangement of long cellular processes emphasizes how these progenitor populations span the tissue and likely contribute to cortical growth and neuron production. The drawing is conceptual rather than quantitative, but it clearly compares the positions and shapes of the main progenitor classes.",
